 \begin{abstract}
-Disagreement among instance segmenters can reflect missing foreground or different partitions of shared foreground. These mechanisms need not identify the same errors. We examine their separation in three-model segmentation of peripelvic fractures on CT. On 40 development scans, we quantify component contributions and test controlled merge, split, omission, boundary, and count-replication perturbations. A separate 100-scan evaluation compares 12 fixed scores using case-clustered bootstrap intervals. Joint-entropy normalization improves the combined score over foreground disagreement and mean entropy for pooled merge/split ranking: area under the risk--coverage curve decreases from 0.4942 and 0.6358 to 0.3311. However, the original partition-only component performs better (0.2720). Supplementary one-to-one and significant-overlap endpoints remove clear evidence of that component's advantage over the combined score. Count-based fusion also lacks a clear development gain. Shared omissions can yield zero disagreement, and fixed-budget review benefits remain uncertain. The findings support component-aware, endpoint-specific evaluation of instance disagreement rather than a universal ranking advantage or demonstrated clinical review savings.
+Disagreement among instance segmenters can reflect missing foreground or different groupings of the same foreground. These mechanisms need not identify the same errors. We study their separation in three-model segmentation of peripelvic fractures on CT. Controlled perturbations and component analysis use 40 development scans; a separate 100-scan evaluation compares 12 fixed scores. Dividing partition disagreement by joint entropy, rather than by the logarithm of voxel count, increases its contribution to the combined score. For pooled merge/split detection, this combined score achieves area under the risk--coverage curve 0.3311, compared with 0.4942 for foreground disagreement and 0.6358 for mean entropy; lower values indicate fewer errors among retained predictions. A partition-only score nevertheless performs better (0.2720). Its advantage is no longer clear under supplementary one-to-one and significant-overlap failure definitions. Unanimously missing bones can receive zero disagreement, and results at fixed review budgets do not establish reliable benefit. These findings show why component controls and explicit failure definitions are necessary when evaluating prediction disagreement for quality control.
 \end{abstract}
