 \section{Results}
 \subsection{Component Contributions and Controlled Responses}
-On the 80 development bones, the median weighted partition term increased from 0.001164 (v1) to 0.032116 (v2). Its median contribution to the corresponding total score increased from 6.96\% to 58.87\%. Raw mean-pair VI was at most $10^{-12}$ in 27/80 bones. Consequently, normalization changes component scale but cannot create partition information where the members already agree.
+On the 80 development bones, changing from voxel-count to joint-entropy normalization increased the median weighted partition term from 0.001164 to 0.032116. Its median share of the corresponding combined score rose from 6.96\% to 58.87\%. Thus a component that contributed little numerically could become a major part of the score without changing the underlying predictions. Raw VI averaged over model pairs was at most $10^{-12}$ in 27/80 bones, however. Normalization therefore changes component scale but cannot create disagreement where the models already give the same grouping.
 
-All 32 phantom assertions passed. The CT-based perturbation study produced 452 eligible responses from 527 rows; 75 merge rows were ineligible because there were insufficient fragments. Identity and ID permutation produced zero disagreement. Merge/split changed partition terms without presence change, whereas the tested missing/boundary interventions changed presence with zero shared-partition VI. All 120 boundary targets met the declared tolerance. Count replication reconstructed 240 development contingencies and verified the expected denominator mechanism (Fig.~\ref{fig:results}A).
+All 32 synthetic-phantom assertions passed. The CT-based perturbation study produced 452 eligible responses from 527 tests; 75 merge tests were ineligible because there were insufficient fragments. Unchanged and permuted labels produced zero disagreement. Merge/split interventions changed partition terms without changing presence, while the tested fragment-removal and boundary interventions changed presence with zero shared-partition VI. These responses confirm that the components distinguish the intended mechanisms under these controlled interventions. All 120 boundary targets met the declared tolerance. Count replication reconstructed 240 development contingencies and confirmed that voxel-count normalization shrinks the partition score despite unchanged fragment proportions (Fig.~\ref{fig:results}A).
 
-Normalized VI also saturated: 11 single-fragment bones had $Q_2=1$ at all three split levels. For merges, the 12 bones eligible at both levels one and two had median $Q_2$ 0.01503 and 0.03302. The four eligible through level three had medians 0.00572, 0.01786, and 0.03235. The response therefore must be interpreted within fixed eligible cohorts and does not universally count structural severity.
+Joint-entropy-normalized VI also saturated: 11 single-fragment bones had $Q_{\rm joint}=1$ at all three split levels. Once saturated, the score cannot distinguish a mild split from a more extensive split. For merges, the 12 bones eligible at both levels one and two had median $Q_{\rm joint}$ 0.01503 and 0.03302. The four eligible through level three had medians 0.00572, 0.01786, and 0.03235. These within-cohort increases show a response to the tested merges, but the saturation result prevents interpreting normalized VI as a universal count of structural severity.
 
