-The configured pooled comparison criterion was met. FA-v2 reduced member-mean structural AURC by 0.16313 versus presence (95\% CI $[-0.21184,-0.11682]$) and 0.30470 versus mean entropy ($[-0.37159,-0.23683]$). Stage~2 risks supported the same comparisons, with differences $-0.12760$ and $-0.28142$, respectively. Femur-only member-mean intervals crossed zero, so the pooled result does not establish a benefit separately in both scan types.
+The joint-entropy combined score met the recorded pooled comparison criterion. Its structural AURC was lower by 0.16313 than presence (95\% CI $[-0.21184,-0.11682]$) and by 0.30470 than mean entropy ($[-0.37159,-0.23683]$). These differences mean that, over the evaluated coverage range, the combined ranking retained fewer merge/split errors than either foreground-based control. Evaluating errors from the additional reference model supported the same comparisons, with differences $-0.12760$ and $-0.28142$, respectively. Femur-only intervals crossed zero, so the pooled result does not establish a benefit separately for both scan types.
 
-Table~\ref{tab:main} shows why these contrasts are insufficient for overall superiority. Partition-v1 achieved held-out structural AURC 0.2720 versus FA-v2's 0.3311; the paired difference favored partition-v1. Hungarian disagreement had the lowest point AURC for the original $1-F_1$ endpoint. Thus the normalization repair improved the combined score against foreground controls but did not make it the strongest detector among the tested scores.
+The partition-only control was stronger than the combined score on the original structural endpoint (Table~\ref{tab:main}). Voxel-normalized partition disagreement achieved AURC 0.2720, compared with 0.3311 for the joint-entropy combined score, and the paired interval favored the partition-only control. A larger partition contribution therefore does not guarantee a better ranking: adding presence can change which errors receive highest priority. Hungarian matching disagreement had the lowest point AURC for the original $1-F_1$ endpoint, showing that the strongest score also depends on whether failure means incorrect grouping or poor instance detection.
 
