-\subsection{Endpoint Dependence and Failed Fusion}
-The supplementary evaluation reconciled all original per-model risks before recomputing matching-based endpoints on the same 140 evaluation CTs. On held-out member-mean risk, FA-v2's disadvantage to partition-v1 was clear for the original M+S definition, but not for one-to-one $1-F_1$ or either significant-overlap definition (Table~\ref{tab:sensitivity}). These changes do not show FA-v2 superiority. They show that the component advantage depends on what is counted as a structural failure. Absolute AURCs across different risk definitions are not changes in segmentation quality.
+\subsection{Dependence on the Failure Definition}
+The supplementary evaluation reproduced the original per-model risks before recomputing alternative endpoints on the same 40 development and 100 evaluation CTs. For the separate evaluation cohort, the joint-entropy combined score was worse than voxel-normalized partition disagreement under the original merge/split definition, but this disadvantage was no longer clear for one-to-one $1-F_1$ or either significant-overlap definition (Table~\ref{tab:sensitivity}). The segmentations and scores stayed fixed; only the definition of failure changed. The result therefore shows endpoint dependence, not improved segmentation or superiority of the combined score under the alternative definitions. The different risk scales also prevent comparing absolute AURCs across endpoint definitions.
 
