-\subsection{Omission and Fixed-Budget Review}
-Case \emph{187/left hip} contained five reference fragments, but all three members and Stage~2 predicted an empty bone. Every ensemble disagreement score was zero, while missing-volume risk was one and M+S risk was zero. In a different held-out example, \emph{302/femur/member 03}, the original internal $F_1$ was one despite two merges, eight splits, and 43.56\% missing reference volume. These examples expose the information and endpoint limits directly.
-
-Across held-out CTs, the complete-omission policy endpoint had only two positive CTs. Guard-plus-partition-v1 sensitivity was 0.5 at 10\% CT review and 1.0 at 20\%, but confidence intervals were undefined because some bootstrap draws contained no positives. At 20\%, the guard reduced retained missing-volume risk by a point estimate of 0.00429, with CI $[-0.01213,0.00810]$. The small positive count and interval crossing zero prevent a reliable review-utility claim. Separately, the original bone-budget supplementary analysis found no clear FA-v2 benefit over presence for retained M+S risk at either budget, and worse missing-volume prioritization.
+\subsection{Shared Omission as an Information Limit}
+In one evaluation example, the left hip contained five reference fragments, but all three ensemble models and the additional reference model predicted an empty bone. Every ensemble disagreement score was zero, while missing-volume risk was one and merge/split risk was zero. Agreement had assigned the lowest uncertainty to a completely missed bone, and a merge/split endpoint alone did not count that omission. This failure requires information beyond disagreement among identical predictions.
 
 \section{Discussion}
-The decomposition resolves two issues that can otherwise be conflated. First, a count-dependent denominator can numerically suppress partition disagreement. The replication test isolates this effect without inference or image resampling. Second, correcting that suppression does not ensure the preferred ordering of clinically relevant failures. Partition-v1 remained stronger on the original structural endpoint, while supplementary matching rules changed the relative evidence. This agrees with problem-aware validation recommendations~\cite{reinke2024,maier2024}: scale, evaluation unit, and failure definition require separate controls.
+The experiments separate score magnitude from failure-detection value. Replication shows that a voxel-count denominator can suppress partition disagreement without any change in fragment proportions. The separate evaluation cohort shows that removing this suppression helps the combined score against foreground controls, while leaving a partition-only control stronger on the original structural endpoint. Supplementary matching rules then change that comparison without changing any prediction. In practice, a quality-control score should therefore be chosen for a stated failure definition, rather than because one component has a larger numerical range. This agrees with problem-aware validation recommendations~\cite{reinke2024,maier2024}: scale, evaluation unit, and failure definition need separate controls.
 
-Disagreement also cannot distinguish unanimous correctness from unanimous error when its inputs are identical. In this implementation even entropy vanishes for an empty predicted ROI. An expected-anatomy rule adds a limited prior, but the observed omission sample is too small to validate a general safety mechanism. More informative quality assessment would need image, anatomical, or independent reference evidence, as illustrated by reference-based approaches~\cite{valindria2017}. A guard appropriate for an expected pelvic bone cannot be transferred unchanged to a dataset containing genuine negative subjects.
+Disagreement cannot distinguish unanimous correctness from unanimous error when its inputs are identical. Even entropy vanishes here for an empty predicted region. An expected-anatomy rule adds a limited prior, but only two evaluation CTs met the complete-omission endpoint, preventing a stable assessment. Fixed-budget analyses also did not establish a reliable review benefit. More informative quality assessment would need image, anatomical, or independent reference evidence, as illustrated by reference-based approaches~\cite{valindria2017}. An expected-bone guard cannot be transferred unchanged to data containing genuine negative subjects.
 
-The main limitations are one segmentation-backend family, only three ensemble members, limited femur precision, and no independent replication of the post-held-out analyses. Pooled bone weighting differs from equal-CT review cost. Prediction cleanup can remove small components while reference annotations retain small fragments. Significant-overlap thresholds were diagnostic choices rather than clinically validated cutoffs. Clinical correction, decision consequences, and reading time were not measured. External RibFrac training is ongoing and supplies no completed external evidence for this manuscript. These limitations favor a diagnostic interpretation over claims of general transfer or clinical savings.
+The main limitations are one segmentation-backend family, only three ensemble members, limited femur precision, and no independent replication of the supplementary endpoint analyses. Pooled bone weighting differs from equal-CT review cost, so pooled ranking gains cannot be translated directly into a percentage of scans saved from review. Prediction cleanup can remove small components while reference annotations retain small fragments. Significant-overlap thresholds were diagnostic choices rather than clinically validated cutoffs. Clinical correction, decision consequences, reading time, and external-dataset transfer were not evaluated. The findings consequently characterize the behavior of disagreement scores in this setting; they do not establish clinical savings or safety across datasets.
 
 \section{Conclusion}
-Separating foreground presence from shared-foreground partition disagreement clarifies both a normalization mechanism and its limits. The combined normalized score improved pooled held-out ranking over presence and entropy, but a partition-only baseline was stronger under the original structural endpoint. That advantage was endpoint-dependent, small learned fusions did not establish improvement, and shared omissions remained a blindspot. Instance-segmentation quality control should therefore report component controls, matching sensitivity, and review-budget outcomes together.
+Joint-entropy normalization improved combined-score ranking over presence and entropy, but voxel-normalized partition disagreement remained stronger under the original structural endpoint. That advantage depended on the failure definition, while shared omissions stayed invisible to disagreement. Component controls, matching sensitivity, and fixed-budget outcomes are therefore needed together when assessing quality control.
 
